  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA few disturbances moving through a mean upper level trough in \nplace across the Great Lakes and Northeast, will bring isolated \nto scattered showers to areas mainly north of I-90 this \nafternoon into this evening and again on Fri. Due to cold air \naloft and fairly steep mid-level lapse rates, some very small \nhail may occur in some low-topped convective cores. It will \nremain breezy this afternoon and again on Fri due to good mixing\nconditions, with west-northwest winds gusting 20-30 mph at \ntimes and especially in favored areas for channeling such as the\nMohawk Valley, Capital District, N. Taconics and Berkshires. \nTemperatures look to be near normal the next few days. High \npressure then builds in Fri night through Sat, providing dry and\nmainly clear conditions along with lighter winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSun P.M. into Sun night, the remnant moisture from what is left\nof post-T.C. Isaias is expected to move into the region from \nthe south/west. Most sources of guidance track a weakened \nsurface cyclone from the mid Atlantic region to just south of \nNYC Sun night into early Mon. While deep layer moisture does \nincrease (PWAT anomalies of +2 to +3 STDEV from the NAEFS), \nforcing from this system should be in a weakened state by the \ntime it reaches the Northeast. While widespread rainfall is \nexpected, amounts should generally be < 1.0\". Latest NBM \nprobabilities for > 1.0\" of rain are only 15-30% from I-90 \nsouth, with the highest probs near the I-84 corridor. So this \nwill be a beneficial rainfall. Given several days of dry weather\npreceding this rainfall along with high confidence in overall \nrainfall amounts of < 2.0\", flooding is not anticipated. The \nrain should end Mon morning, as the progressive system quickly \ntracks east off of the coast and surface ridging builds in Mon \nafternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cold front is expected to move across the region next Tue, \nwhich looks to bring some showers to much of the area. Behind \nthe front, the coolest air mass of the season thus far may\npotentially be ushered in as a large area of high pressure \nbuilds east across the Great Lakes and into the Northeast. High \ntemperatures could run 5-10 degrees below normal Wed and Thu, \nalthough dry conditions are expected. Before the high moves in, \ngusty northwest winds will likely occur on Wed.",
